# vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2_planning.py

The todo-list planning prompts that Stage 2 installs through `TodoListMiddleware` when `step2.planning` is on and graph tools are available. The model owns breadth and has no sub-agents.

Source: `vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2_planning.py` (lines 1-67)
Executor: model (prompt definitions)
Called from: `vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/session.py` _threatmodel_call

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2_planning.py:19-54
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2_planning.py:19-54
PLAN_SYSTEM_PROMPT {
  Constraints {
    "## Planning is mandatory here"
    "Your FIRST action is `write_todos`. Do not emit the threat model before you have written a plan and worked it."
    "Every threat you name has to rest on code you actually opened, and every threat you fail to name is invisible to every later stage — this stage sets the ceiling on what the whole scan can find."
    "You have no sub-agents. The todo list IS your coverage guarantee, and it is the only thing standing between a directory and being silently unread."
    Every plan covers at least {
      1. "Partition the FILE INVENTORY into directory groups and write one todo per group, so the plan itself accounts for every file in scope." "The inventory is already in this prompt — partition it, do not go and re-enumerate it with `ls` or Glob."
      2. "Work the groups in order of how likely they are to hide a threat nothing else will find: templates and views first, then container/CI/IaC, then models and persistence, then utils. Request handlers last"
      3. "Open the entry points and confirm which are reachable unauthenticated."
      4. "Establish module structure and blast radius from the graph rather than from directory names" using GraphContext, GraphImpact, GraphCommunityOf, GraphSinksInCommunity; "One `GraphContext` call answers what several Reads would."
      5. "Read the container, CI and infrastructure files — `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose*.yml`, IaC, `.github/**`. Secrets and deployment posture live there and nowhere else."
      6. "Read the templates and client-side files. An unescaped interpolation of stored data is a finding, and nothing else in the pipeline will look for it."
      7. "Give every threat a `surface_files` list of real paths taken from what you actually read."
      8. "Reconcile: subtract every path you named from the inventory and account for what is left, either with a new threat or a one-line reason in `open_questions`. Emit \"unreviewed: <n> file(s) — <reason>\" when any remain."
    }
    "Mark each todo in_progress as you start it and completed only when genuinely done. Add todos when what you read opens something new. Only once every todo is completed do you emit the threat model."
  }
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2_planning.py:56-62
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2_planning.py:56-62
PLAN_TOOL_DESCRIPTION {
  // description of the write_todos tool
  Constraints {
    "Record and update your threat-modelling plan. Call this FIRST, before any other tool, then keep it current"
    "This is not optional and not only for long tasks."
    "Statuses are \"pending\", \"in_progress\" and \"completed\"."
    "Never call this tool twice in one turn."
  }
}
// external: vvaharness/backends/harness/deepagents/evidence_gate.py — the code-side check of these instructions. When s2/session.py installs EvidenceGate (step2.require_graph on with graph tools, or a deepagents route with a non-empty inventory), a final structured answer is sent back while todos are outstanding, required graph tools were never called, or a required tool call is missing, up to step2.require_graph_rejections (default 2) times, after which the gate relents. Without the gate, nothing in code checks that the plan was followed.
